<h1>jump list</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A variant of <a href="doublyLinkedList.html"><em>doubly linked list</em></a> with items in sorted order and having two levels of additional links that span geometrically increasing distances.  For a  list with n items, the next level is a link from item i,  1 &le; i &le; n - &lfloor; n <sup>1/3</sup>&rfloor;, to  item i + &lfloor; i <sup>1/3</sup>&rfloor;.  At the top level, items 1&sup3;, 2&sup3;, 3&sup3;, &hellip;,  &lfloor; n <sup>1/3</sup>&rfloor; &sup3; have backward links, that is, there is a link from item i&sup3;,  1 &lt; i &le; &lfloor; n <sup>1/3</sup>&rfloor; &sup3;, to  item (i-1)&sup3;.   Search, insert, and delete are <a href="bigOnotation.html"><em>O(n<sup>1/3</sup>)</em></a> worst case.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="skiplist.html"><em>skip list</em></a>, <a href="dictionary.html"><em>dictionary</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The data structure can also be seen as a <a href="binarySearchTree.html"><em>binary search tree</em></a> with additional links.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Arne Andersson</strong> and <strong>Thomas Ottmann</strong>, <em>New Tight Bounds on Uniquely Represented Dictionaries</em>, SIAM Journal of Computing, 24(5):1091-1103, 1995.
</p>

